@Transactional — Isolation & Propagation

These two attributes control different things:

- Isolation   → how concurrent transactions see each other’s data
- Propagation → what happens when one transactional method calls another

@Transactional(
    isolation = Isolation.READ_COMMITTED,
    propagation = Propagation.REQUIRED
)
public void transfer() { ... }

────────────────────────────────────────
1. Transaction Isolation

Isolation = visibility rules between concurrent transactions.

Without enough isolation you can get:

1️⃣ Dirty Read
T2 reads data T1 changed but has not committed yet.
If T1 rolls back → T2 used invalid data.

T1: UPDATE account SET balance = 900 WHERE id = 1;  (not committed)
T2: SELECT balance → 900
T1: ROLLBACK

2️⃣ Non-Repeatable Read
Same row read twice in T1; value changes because T2 committed an UPDATE/DELETE.

T1: SELECT balance ... → 1000
T2: UPDATE ... COMMIT
T1: SELECT balance ... → 900

3️⃣ Phantom Read
Same query twice in T1; result set changes because T2 inserted/deleted matching rows.

T1: SELECT * FROM orders WHERE amount > 1000;  → 5 rows
T2: INSERT matching row + COMMIT
T1: same SELECT → 6 rows

────────────────────────────────────────
Isolation Levels

| Level            | Dirty | Non-repeatable | Phantom (ANSI idea) | Notes |
| ---------------- | ----- | -------------- | ------------------- | ----- |
| READ UNCOMMITTED | Yes   | Yes            | Yes                 | Lowest |
| READ COMMITTED   | No    | Yes            | Yes                 | Common default (Postgres/Oracle/SQL Server) |
| REPEATABLE READ  | No    | No             | Possible (ANSI)     | MySQL InnoDB default |
| SERIALIZABLE     | No    | No             | No                  | Strongest; least concurrency |

Interview nuance (MySQL InnoDB):
- ANSI: REPEATABLE READ may still allow phantoms.
- InnoDB REPEATABLE READ uses MVCC (+ gap locks for many locking reads) and often prevents phantoms in practice.
- SERIALIZABLE ≈ transactions behave as if run one after another (more locking / less concurrency).

Higher isolation → safer, usually slower / more contention.

────────────────────────────────────────
2. Transaction Propagation

Propagation = how transactions nest when Method A (TX) calls Method B (TX).

Method A
   └── Method B   ← join A? new TX? no TX?

Must call B through another Spring bean/proxy (self-invocation bypasses propagation).

────────────────────────────────────────
Propagation Types

1️⃣ REQUIRED (default)
- Existing TX → join it
- None → create new
- If B fails → whole shared TX can roll back

2️⃣ REQUIRES_NEW
- Always start a new TX; suspend outer
- B failure rolls back only B; A can still commit
- Typical: audit/logging that must save even if business TX fails

3️⃣ NESTED
- Nested TX / savepoint inside current TX
- Inner rollback can roll back to savepoint; outer may continue
- Needs JDBC savepoint support

4️⃣ SUPPORTS
- Join if TX exists; else run without TX

5️⃣ NOT_SUPPORTED
- Always non-transactional; suspend existing TX

6️⃣ MANDATORY
- Must join existing TX; else exception

7️⃣ NEVER
- Must run without TX; else exception

────────────────────────────────────────
Isolation vs Propagation

| Feature  | Isolation                                         | Propagation                                              |
| -------- | ------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------- |
| Purpose  | Concurrent visibility / anomalies                 | Nested method TX boundaries                              |
| About    | Multiple users/transactions at once               | A calls B in same app                                    |
| Example  | Dirty / non-repeatable / phantom                  | REQUIRED, REQUIRES_NEW, NEVER                            |

────────────────────────────────────────
Short Interview Answer

Isolation defines how concurrent transactions see each other’s data and which read anomalies are allowed.
Propagation defines whether a called method joins the current transaction, starts a new one, or refuses to run in a transaction.
In Spring both are configured on @Transactional.
